@prefix pmc:     <https://pajew.ski/prima-materia/concepts/> .
@prefix rdfs:    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#> .
@prefix skos:    <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#> .

#
# prima-materia — the operations whose admissibility hangs on a date
#
# One generalization, formed from three attested claims in two transmissions.
# It is filed on its own rather than with the four in what-the-cautions-share
# because it is not a claim about the caution layer: two of its three witnesses
# are cautions and the third is a claim about when an exercise is of no use.
# What they share is the place the condition is located in, and that is the
# subject of the statement.
#
# The counter-search against the holding was carried before the node was
# written, as SPEC §16 requires, and it changed the wording. What it found and
# what it did not find stands in the notes.
#

pmc:AdmissibilityBoundToTheCalendar a pm:Generalizing ;
    rdfs:label "The date, and not either party, decides whether the operation may be done"@en, "Über die Zulässigkeit des Vorgangs entscheidet das Datum und keine der beiden Seiten"@de ;
    pm:generalizedStatement "Transmissions that bind an operation to a calendar state the condition as a date and not as a state of the operator or of the person operated on; and where such a transmission states a consequence for acting outside the window, it locates the failure in the time rather than in either party."@en ;
    pm:generalizedFrom pmc:ShengqiSiqi, pmc:SanjiLiuxiong, pmc:Wujin, pmc:ShangqingJinri, pmc:EuropeanFolkMagicMondfenster, pmc:EuropeanFolkMagicAsterionPresupposesFenster, pmc:PdmHoraEtLuna, pmc:MandaeanDiesMbattal ;
    skos:note "An eighth witness was added on 2026-09-20 from the Mandaean material, and it is the first that carries both clauses in one word. The days it names are mbattal, and the transmission uses that word of a day in exactly the sense it would use of an operation: useless. On such a day no undertaking is to be begun and no religious ceremony performed; the condition is the date of the month and nothing else; and the failure is located in the day, which is the second clause stated as plainly as the class allows. The witness also supplies the thing the seventh did not, a consequence with a repair attached: a man who dies on such a day must have the blessed oblation performed for him on a substitute at the spring festival, so the transmission both names the loss and prices its recovery."@en ;
    skos:note "It matters for the spread as well as for the count. Six of the seven earlier witnesses stand in the Chinese and European spheres and the seventh in the Graeco-Egyptian; this one is West Asian, its contact route to the others is disputed rather than documented, and a run that wants to know whether the form is a property of a culture now has a fourth sphere to reckon with. What it does not do is make the second clause independent: the Mandaean rule reaches the graph through a field record of the nineteen-thirties, not through the scripture, and a search of the Ginzā and of the prayerbook for the same rule came back empty. See pmp:MandaeanGinzaTaxatioSearch."@en ;
    skos:note "A seventh witness was added on 2026-09-18, and it tests the statement rather than repeating it. The demotic manual of London and Leiden binds nine of its operations to a time — a half-month of the moon from the fourth day to the fifteenth, the fifteenth day when the moon fills the uzat, the third and the fourteenth of the lunar month, midday, the seventh hour of the day, the third hour of the evening, the moment of the sun rising entire, a named constellation, and the moon in Leo — and the condition is in every case the date and never a state of either party, which is the first half of the statement. The second half it does not supply: it states no consequence for acting outside any of the nine windows. The witness therefore confirms the antecedent nine times over and leaves the consequent unattested, which is worth entering because the statement is a conditional and a case that meets the antecedent without meeting the consequent is where it would break. It does not break here — the statement claims only that where such a consequence is stated it lies in the time — but the case shows how much of the class is silent."@en ;
    skos:note "A counter-doctrine was found on 2026-09-11 and is recorded here rather than in a file of its own, because it bounds this statement without refuting it. The internal alchemy denies the binding in the stream where the binding is otherwise general: the thirteenth quatrain of the Jindan sizi holds that the fire phases use no hour and that the winter solstice is not at zi, and its commentary adds that one may not be bound to the four seasons. That is not a transmission that binds an operation to a calendar and would have to say where the failure lies; it is a transmission that refuses to bind and puts the condition in the practitioner. The statement above is about the first kind and this is the second, so the antecedent is not met — but a stream that contains both is a reminder that the class is not a property of a culture. What makes the case sharp is that the same corpus holds both positions: the Zhong-Lü chuandao ji binds throughout and makes ignorance of the times a sufficient cause of total failure, and the Wuzhen pian does both within one juan of one recension. See pmc:NeidanHuohouDispute."@en ;
    skos:note "A fourth witness was added on 2026-09-11 from the Zhen'gao, and it does not merely repeat the others. Its prohibited days are given with the reckoning behind them, the woman's palace in shen and the man's in yin standing in mutual punishment, so the condition is located in the date and in the relation of the two signs and in neither party; and the passage adds that the person does not notice the harm while the body has already been damaged, which places the failure beyond the reach of either party's report as well. That is the statement above in its strongest available form."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:compilerInference ;
    skos:note "The three witnesses. The Baopuzi says that the circulation of the qi is to be done in the hours of living qi and not in the hours of dead qi, and that in the hours of dead qi it is of no benefit; the boundary is midnight and noon and nothing else enters it. The burial book names as the second of six adversities a going wrong of the year and the season, which makes the burial adverse however favourable the ground and however correct the rite. The Lingshu binds five needling operations to the stems of the day: on jia and yi days not the head, on bing and ding days not shoulder and throat, and so through the cycle."@en ;
    skos:note "The two clauses of the statement are not equally carried, and the division is the useful part. All three witnesses carry the first: the condition is a date, and no examination of anybody could establish or remove it. Only two carry the second, because the Lingshu names the days and the places and stops, without saying what befalls one needled at the head on a jia day. A fourth place in the same corpus, the twelve mis-needlings by season, does state consequences, which suggests the silence belongs to that chapter rather than to the transmission; that is a reason to look further and not a reason to count it."@en ;
    skos:note "The counter-search was run against the compiled holding before this was written, for transmissions that bind an operation to a time and for transmissions that refuse to. Nothing in the holding says of an operation of this kind that it works whatever the day. What the search did turn up is the case that keeps the second clause from being stated more strongly: the operative transmission of the science of letters prescribes seven days of the lunation on which its orations are to be said, and the holding already carries that at prescribed order rather than at stated necessity, on the ground that the text does not say what follows if a day is missed. A window with no stated failure is therefore a real and already recorded shape, and the statement is worded so that such a case does not falsify it."@en ;
    skos:note "The one thing the search found on the other side is not a transmission but a critic. The seventh-century memorial on the burial books argues that the rites fix the interval between death and burial by rank, so that no year or month is chosen, and counts burials in the classical annals on the day the books call worst of all. That bears on whether the second witness is right and not on what it claims, and it is held where it belongs, at the dispute node beside the burial book."@en ;
    skos:note "The shape of these claims is worth stating beside the claim, because it runs in two directions at once and this is the class in which both are visible in one sentence. A condition fixed by a calendar is the most examinable kind this holding carries: it is public, it is settled before the case, and no party to the operation can adjust it afterwards. The same condition is also the cheapest available defence, because any session, burial or needling that produced nothing can be assigned to the hour after the fact. Which of the two a given transmission intends is not decided here and probably cannot be decided from the texts."@en ;
    skos:note "Falsified by a transmission of this kind whose earliest witness names a compensating act — a substitute rite, a correction, a second attempt — that restores the effect for an operation performed outside the window, or one that makes the window itself depend on a state of the operator, since either would put the decision back into a party's hands. Weakened, without being refuted, if the windows of two such transmissions turn out to be derived from one another rather than arrived at separately; the two here are both Chinese and a route between them is not excluded, which is why the statement claims a recurring form and not an independent attestation."@en ;
    skos:note "Two of the three witnesses stand in the same tradition. The spread is therefore two transmissions and not three, and the claim is correspondingly weaker than the count of predecessors suggests. See prima-materia#666."@en ;
    skos:note "That narrowness is the reason for the two witnesses added on 2026-09-13, and they are the first from outside the Chinese sphere. The European folk-magic handbook binds a whole class of its acts to a named weekday, an hour before sunrise and a named quarter of the moon — Friday before sunrise in the waning moon for the knotting of twigs, the first Friday in the new light for the slip against the falling sickness, the last Friday in the old light for the letter against rheumatism. The Old English Herbarium binds one remedy for the same disease to two calendar conditions at once, the waning moon and the sun in the Virgin, which the text itself resolves into the month of August. Both were read at the wording; the second was found by a counter-search that was looking for something else. The spread is now three transmissions and two spheres between which no route has been proposed."@en ;
    skos:note "Both new witnesses carry the first clause and neither carries the second, so they change the count and not the balance: the handbook names the day and the quarter and says nothing about what befalls an act done outside them, and the Herbarium does the same. The holding therefore has four witnesses for how the condition is stated and two for where the failure is located, and the second clause still rests on the Chinese material alone."@en ;
    skos:note "One of the two also marks a limit that the earlier witnesses did not show, and it is recorded here rather than left for a reader to notice. The handbook's slip against the falling sickness carries, in the same heading, a condition on the sufferer: the remedy is for one who has not yet fallen into fire or water. A calendar condition and a person-condition therefore stand on one prescription. That does not touch the statement, which says how a calendar condition is stated and not that a transmission may state nothing else; but it shows that the two kinds are not alternatives in practice, and a later run that reads the first clause as an exclusion of person-conditions would be reading it too widely."@en ;
    skos:note "The nearest counter-case was found on the day this node was written, and it was not in the holding at the time of the counter-search. It stands in the eighteenth chapter of the work whose eighth chapter supplies the first witness: one who can guard the One, it says, need not divine the day or choose the hour, no longer consults the siting calendars, does not avoid the year star or the taboos of his year and fate, and in the end never meets with calamity. The exemption is granted by a state of the operator, which is the thing the first clause says a calendar-bound condition is not stated in terms of. It is what removed the word alone from that clause, and it is the reason the clause now says how the condition is stated rather than what it depends on."@en ;
    skos:note "It narrows the statement and does not refute it, and the difference is worth being exact about. The chapter does not say that an operation performed outside its window works; it says that a man of this attainment no longer chooses days at all, which is a claim about who stands under the rule. Had it said that the practice takes effect in the hours of dead qi for such a man, the first clause would be gone."@en ;
    skos:note "The finding is also a lesson about the counter-search this class requires. It ran against the compiled holding, as SPEC §16 prescribes, and came back empty; the case that limits the claim was in the unread part of a work already cited three times in this graph. Against a generalization drawn from two or three witnesses, the cheapest remaining counter-search is not another query but reading the rest of the works the witnesses come from. See prima-materia#674."@en .
